{"concern_id":"binding-name-collision-stale-uppercase","evidence":[{"artifact_path":".gnd-planning/milestones/ossie-relational-bundle/notes/close-delta.md","location":"Plan amendments, item 4 (A14)","observation":"\"Identifiers are quoted in Ossie's normalized form: upper-cased only when regular. Plan r2 and OB3 as merged upper-cased every name\". The binding reader was not brought into line. src/gnd/export/physical_binding.py:244-251 still says \"# binding:name-collision. Expressions quote and uppercase every name.\" and refuses whenever name.upper() is equal. representation-format.md (around line 2373) repeats this: \"Two tables, or two columns of one table, that are equal after uppercasing are refused.\" Under the A14 rule, non-regular names such as `fin.refunds` and `FIN.Refunds` are quoted as written (ossie_relational.py:115) and are distinct Ossie identifiers, yet the binding is refused with binding:name-collision. The check never under-refuses, because two regular names with equal upper() still collide. physical_binding.py is listed in close-results as carrying no review opinion."}],"recommendation":"Compare by the normalized form _quoted produces: upper() when is_regular_identifier(name), else the name as written. Update the code comment and the normative sentence, and add a case where two non-regular names differ only in case and are accepted. Alternatively, keep the stricter rule on purpose and say in the normative text that it is deliberately stricter than Ossie.","relevance":"hardening","severity":"low","title":"The binding name-collision rule still assumes every name is upper-cased, so after A14 it refuses distinct non-regular names"}
